{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg8xWgXzgt4BJuatNjko","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Treat KCoF₃ as high-spin octahedral Co²⁺ (d⁷) with a ⁴T₁g(F) ground state. I fitted Dq and B to the three quartet bands, then found C from the doublet band. The results are **Dq ≈ 815, B ≈ 878 and C ≈ 3 980 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**1. Dq and B from the spin-allowed (quartet) bands**\n\n- A quick check: the ⁴T₂g and ⁴A₂g energies differ by exactly 10Dq. That gives 15 200 − 7 150 = 8 050 cm⁻¹, so Dq ≈ 805 cm⁻¹.\n- Three bands with two parameters is more data than needed, so the values can't all be met exactly. I therefore did a least-squares fit of all three quartet bands to the d⁷ matrices.\n- The quartet energies relative to the ground state don't depend on C, so this fit gives Dq and B alone:\n  - **Dq ≈ 815 cm⁻¹** (10Dq ≈ 8 150 cm⁻¹)\n  - **B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹**\n- How well the fit matches the measured bands:\n\n| Band | Observed (cm⁻¹) | Calculated (cm⁻¹) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ⁴T₂g | 7 150 | 7 086 |\n| ⁴A₂g | 15 200 | 15 231 |\n| ⁴T₁g(P) | 19 200 | 19 199 |\n\n- The root-mean-square error is about 41 cm⁻¹.\n\n**2. C from the spin-forbidden ²T₁g(2) band**\n\n- With Dq and B fixed, the calculated ²T₁g(2) energy depends almost linearly on C, rising about 3.0 cm⁻¹ for each 1 cm⁻¹ increase in C:\n  - At C = 4 499 cm⁻¹ (the tool's default value), it comes out at 22 690 cm⁻¹.\n  - At C = 4 000 cm⁻¹, it comes out at 21 193 cm⁻¹.\n- To match the observed 21 130 cm⁻¹, C must be about 4 000 − 63/3.0 ≈ **3 980 cm⁻¹**.\n- This gives C/B ≈ 4.5, which is typical for Co²⁺.\n- B is about 80 % of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (about 1 120 cm⁻¹). That modest reduction fits the fairly ionic bonding expected with fluoride ligands.\n\n{\"Dq\": 815, \"B\": 878, \"C\": 3980, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq and B were fitted to the three quartet bands and C was set by the ²T₁g(2) band.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequenc